Make the Problems rules table a labelled keyboard region, and bind the sheet's paint claims

Two findings from the round-9 independent review of #52, filed rather than
fixed there because an APPROVE ends the cycle.

Stadiora/Aria#10459: the box the rules table scrolls inside had no tabindex,
no role, no name and no focus offset. At 375px it reported clientWidth 343
against scrollWidth 967, and for a non-owner every switch inside it is
disabled, so it held nothing focusable at all. It now carries tabindex="0",
role="region" and the table's own screen-reader caption as its name, with the
focus ring pulled inside its edge -- the same three attributes and the same
one CSS line as pane-releases.js's .tbl-scroll and settings.js's tableWrap().

Stadiora/Aria#10460: two prose claims the code did not support.

  - The widening pin's second probe read the pane's own #fCategory while
    three sentences called it the shell's range control. The probe is now
    #fRange, which makes the sentences true and is the stronger test: it
    asserts the node is outside the .filters-pane slot this pane owns, and
    counts the reads so an unchanged selection cannot pass the assertion
    vacuously.
  - The stylesheet's docblock said "No new colour value. Every rule here
    paints in a token aria.css declares" while .av mixed var(--cyan) toward a
    raw `black`. The docblock now states the exception and why, and the test
    file derives all of it: every non-token atom the sheet paints with is
    pinned per site, the avatar ink is measured against the darkest point of
    its own gradient in both themes, and every aria.css token is tried in that
    mix to show none could stand in.
